WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.229 --> 00:00:07.290
The word addiction comes from the Latin word
for "enslaved by".

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:13.809
And rightly so, millions of people are enslaved
by their fix, whether it be controlled substances,

00:00:13.809 --> 00:00:16.500
food, alcohol or even the internet.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:21.089
We all have that one thing that we just can't
live without.

00:00:21.089 --> 00:00:33.620
But why do we become addicted, and what can
you do to cure it?

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:39.460
Addiction is a serious global concern, and
in some countries addiction rates, especially

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:44.550
to illicit drugs are a serious and growing
concern.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:50.769
In the United States for example, substance
abuse is one of the leading causes of death,

00:00:50.769 --> 00:00:56.910
with a substantial 10% of the American population
addicted to illicit drugs.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:03.390
However, Iran has the world's highest drug
addiction rate, there's a huge heroin epidemic

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:05.250
in the country.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:11.590
Whereas the United Kingdom is the world's
largest abuser of alcohol and France is the

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:14.930
country most addicted to prescription drugs.

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:21.110
For a long time scientists believed that only
alcohol and powerful drugs could cause addiction.

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:27.110
But using advanced Neuroimaging techniques
it has recently become apparent that a whole

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:34.500
array of pleasurable activities, such as gambling,
shopping, sex, video games, the internet and

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.980
many others, can also trigger strong addictions.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:43.560
Also, in the 1930s when research on Addiction
first began.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:48.610
It was believed that addiction was caused
by a lack of willpower and it only affected

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:52.890
weak minded or morally flawed individuals.

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:57.810
So instead of helping addicts to overcome
their addiction they would often punish them

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:03.210
instead, unsurprisingly this didn't work.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:05.950
Recently the scientific consensus has changed.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:11.200
Today addiction is recognised as a chronic
disease, that actual changes the structure

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.299
and function of the brain.

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:18.379
The more we exercise our addiction and take
pleasure from it the more it takes over our

00:02:18.379 --> 00:02:24.459
brain and changes it, so that we crave that
thing even more in the future.

00:02:24.459 --> 00:02:27.810
Addiction really is a vicious cycle.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:33.469
Different addictions show different symptoms,
for example a tobacco addiction has slightly

00:02:33.469 --> 00:02:36.920
different symptoms to a gambling addiction.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:42.640
Someone addicted to tobacco or any other substance,
will usually experience strong physiological

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:46.959
symptoms such as headaches, nausea and fatigue.

00:02:46.959 --> 00:02:52.590
Whereas people addicted to activities such
as gambling will experience more mental symptoms,

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:59.280
such as being overly secretive, lying to their
friends and family, and continuing to gamble

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.900
even when they can't afford to.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:08.500
However, despite the difference in symptoms,
what actually causes these addictions is thought

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:10.450
to be extremely similar.

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:16.849
The brain registers all pleasures in the same
way, no matter what causes it, whether it

00:03:16.849 --> 00:03:20.959
be substances, drink, food or activities.

00:03:20.959 --> 00:03:25.659
The set of chemical processes in the brain
that make you feel that pleasure, are the

00:03:25.659 --> 00:03:27.099
same.

00:03:27.099 --> 00:03:33.560
In all cases, it is caused by the release
of dopamine in the nucleus accumbens, a cluster

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:37.159
of nerve cells underneath the cerebral cortex.

00:03:37.159 --> 00:03:42.770
This area of the brain is so closely linked
to pleasure that neuroscientists call it the

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:44.810
brain's pleasure centre.

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:50.650
The likelihood of us getting addicted to a
particular thing all depends on how fast it

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:53.829
increases the dopamine levels in our brain.

00:03:53.829 --> 00:03:59.180
For example, drinking coffee releases causes
our nucleus accumbens to release dopamine

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:06.669
slowly and steadily, so it's highly unlikely
to cause addiction, although not impossible.

00:04:06.669 --> 00:04:13.529
Whereas drugs such as nicotine, meth, cocaine
and heroin, all cause a powerful and very

00:04:13.529 --> 00:04:16.769
rapid release of dopamine in our brain.

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:22.510
When this happens another part of our brain
called the hippocampus, which is responsible

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:29.500
for forming memories, creates new memories
of the rapid sense of satisfaction, creating

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:36.960
an eternal link in our brains between the
drug and a feeling of intense pleasure.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:42.600
Since our body likes to feel pleasure, another
part of our brain, the amygdala.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:49.400
Creates a permanent positive response to certain
stimuli related to our addiction.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.360
Such as the smell of cigarette smoke.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:56.940
So when the addict smells this they will crave
a cigarette.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:02.730
This is why regular smokers find the smell
of cigarette smoke intensely pleasurable,

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:05.490
whereas non-smokers find it unpleasant.

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:13.030
But by far the most dangerous part of addiction
is how our brains builds up a tolerance.

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:20.160
In nature dopamine is hard to come by and
requires a significant effort to achieve.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:24.090
Whereas drugs and other addictions provide
us with a shortcut.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:29.850
Drugs can provide our brain with up to 10
times as much dopamine as anything that can

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:31.980
be found in nature.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:38.140
Our brain hasn't yet developed to withstand
such an overload of dopamine release.

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:43.880
So to protect the our brain receptors from
becoming overloaded, over time our brain turns

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:50.260
down the volume on our brain receptors, making
them significantly less receptive to future

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:52.240
dopamine releases.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:57.870
This has a devastating affect, because the
addict will now have to increase their dosage

00:05:57.870 --> 00:06:02.290
to feel the same amount of pleasure they did
the previous time.

00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:08.820
And so, every time an addict indulges their
fix, their next dosage will have to be increased

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:11.200
to achieve the same high.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:17.080
This is a vicious cycle that gets exponentially
worse, until the body can't take the dosage

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:20.260
anymore and it breaks down.

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:23.730
So what can you do to overcome addiction?

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:27.810
Firstly you need to acknowledge that you have
an addiction.

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:32.940
Many people are in denial about their addiction
or simply don't realise that they actually

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:34.190
have one.

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:38.531
There's three simple questions you can ask
yourself to test for an addiction, if you

00:06:38.531 --> 00:06:43.680
answer yes to any of the three questions you
may be addicted.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:49.600
Question 1: Do you use a substance or do something
that brings you pleasure a lot more often

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:52.560
that you did in the past?

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:58.640
Question 2: Do you experience withdrawal symptoms
when you haven't used the substance or engaged

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:02.100
in the activity for a while?

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:07.600
Question 3: Have you ever lied to someone
about your use of the substance or the extent

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:09.190
of your behaviour?

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:15.630
Okay, so you've established you have an addiction,
what do you do next?

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:18.880
Next you establish why you want to quit.

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:23.650
Write down a list of all the reasons why you
should quit and look at it every day.

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:30.310
For example, if you're addicted to smoking,
you could write, "I will have healthier lungs".

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:34.090
You now have a set of goals to work towards.

00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:40.960
Next set a date that you will quit for good,
you can set it in 2 weeks or 2 months time,

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:47.910
but whatever you do, don't set it at tomorrow
and tell yourself, "tomorrow I quit forever,

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:50.470
I'm going cold turkey."

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:56.920
Because it's been scientifically proven, going
cold turkey doesn't work, at least not for

00:07:56.920 --> 00:07:57.920
the majority.

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:06.420
Hoards of research over the years has demonstrated
that only 3% of people who go cold turkey

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:09.450
actually quit, the other 97% relapse pretty
quickly.

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:12.800
You should instead, wean your body off your
addiction slowly.

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:17.540
Now you have a goal and a set time to do it
in.

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:20.570
Next you need to identify your triggers.

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:27.200
A trigger is anything that makes you think
of your addiction and might cause you to relapse.

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:32.810
For example, if you're addiction is alcohol,
passing a certain bar may be a trigger for

00:08:32.810 --> 00:08:33.810
you.

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:38.839
Once you've identified your triggers you need
to eliminate them from your life, avoid them

00:08:38.839 --> 00:08:43.120
at all costs to stop any unwanted temptation.

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:49.319
And finally, and possibly the most important
step, you need to find something to replace

00:08:49.319 --> 00:08:50.319
your addiction.

00:08:50.319 --> 00:08:55.399
Your addiction has been a big part of your
life up to now, so once it's gone from your

00:08:55.399 --> 00:09:01.850
life you may find it difficult to fill the
time, and that's dangerous, because when you're

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:05.550
bored, you're at the most risk of relapsing.

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:11.310
So you need to find something to fill that
void, try to start a project, take up outdoor

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:14.240
sports, or simply go for a walk.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:20.259
The more you get involved with a new, positive
activity the more you will forget about the

00:09:20.259 --> 00:09:31.349
thing that you used to be addicted to.

